 204: *** THE LATCH-KEYED ACCEPT SHRINK (the K-CLASS) AND ITS CLOSURE -- repair 13, and it is the reason this probe grew
 205: from 157 rows to 188. ***
 206: 
 207: An accept guard grown with a conjunct on THIS FIX'S OWN LATCH -- `&& !(<cell> && (d->warned & CRIME_W_LEN))' -- declines
 208: SILENTLY at that cell once any refusal has happened, because the latch is exactly what suppresses the announce.  Three
 209: instances were MEASURED at 157/157 with a guest loss on the 157-row instrument (closing pass 7, opus_close7_20260915/
 210: OPUS_REPORT.md): K1 at INTMASK 4W, K2 at the REV 4-byte cell, K3 at CONTROL 8R.  The value rows at THOSE THREE
 211: CELLS are measured with the latch CLEAR, so none of them could see it.
 212: *** THIS SENTENCE READ "EVERY value row this probe had was measured with the latch CLEAR" AND IS FALSE -- codex,
 213: closing pass 8, naming `imask keeps', `sb keeps', `sh keeps' and the TIME controls as rows that already operate
 214: AFTER a refusal.  IT IS THE SECOND TIME I HAVE WRITTEN IT: the close-7 seat corrected the same over-generalisation
 215: in MAINLOOP_HARVEST.md (45 sessions, 72 post-refusal accepted accesses in eleven of them), and I reintroduced it
 216: here while writing repair 13's contract.  The true statement is the narrow one above, plus the derived bucket: of
 217: the 31 guest-visible ACCEPTS entries, EIGHT are already pinned post-latch (session_map2.py).  A sentence corrected
 218: once and rewritten later is worse than one never corrected, because the correction is on record and looks applied. ***
 219: 
 220: *** THE GRADE IS FLOOR, NOT CLAUSE (e), and that OVERTURNED THE PRIOR RULING (FABLE_C5_RULING_20260915.md, S-A.7;
 221: FABLE_C4_RULING's RA3 corrected in place).  The reason is in the fix's own diff: the width dispatch's `else' arm READS
 222: `d->warned', in a hunk this diff ADDS -- so the latch is a device-state lvalue the fix's own repair path reads, which
 223: the pass-11 floor definition and the M_ICLR precedent make a floor axis. ***
 224: 
 225: THE ENUMERATION is the four-axis product {latch clear, set} x {len 4, 8} x {read, write} x {the 7 labels the switch
 226: distinguishes: REV, CONTROL, INTSTAT, INTMASK, DOG, TIME, default} = 56 cells.
 227: *** THIS SENTENCE READ "The latch-CLEAR half is table 10's twelve S_* silent bypasses" AND IS FALSE TWICE -- the glm
 228: seat, closing pass 8, and it is the sharpest finding of that pass.  (i) THE LATCH-CLEAR HALF IS 28 CELLS AND TWELVE IS
 229: TWELVE.  (ii) Materially, and by this round's OWN evidence: close-7's E2 proved TIME 4W/4R guest-visible on a
 230: LATCH-CLEAR observation ("free-running ld;sw;ld reads 0xc6 then 0x108 on base and 0x0 under the bypass" -- no refusal
 231: in that sequence), and TIME 4W/4R are NOT among the twelve, whose only TIME cell is 8W.  So the half provably contains
 232: a guest-visible cell outside the set that was said to close it. ***
 233: *** AND THE CONSEQUENCE IS A CANDIDATE ESCAPE, THE MIRROR OF THE K-CLASS: repair 13 rows TIME 4W/4R ONLY POST-LATCH --
 234: every `p *' session opens with a refused lb, which is the whole point of them -- so a bypass conditioned on the latch
 235: being CLEAR has NO row running in its state at that cell.  The amended catching clause above ("CAUGHT ... AND THE
 236: CATCHING ROW RUNS IN THE BYPASS'S OWN LATCH STATE") therefore fails for it by its own words.  The owed row is the same
 237: GAPPED shape MINUS the opening refused lb.  Under measurement by the closing-pass-8 measuring seat; if it escapes, it
 238: is an L2 floor cell and repair 14 owes the latch-CLEAR half the same treatment the latch-SET half got. ***  **THE ROWS OWED ARE NOT ALL 28 LATCH-SET MUTANTS AND NOT ALL 56 CELLS**: a mutant at an
 239: equivalent or already-pinned cell teaches nothing and a row at a cell no mutant reaches is unfalsifiable, so the FLOOR
 240: is the INTERSECTION.  Over the 46 ACCEPTS entries (numbered 1-46 in ACCEPTS order), AS REPAIR 13 PARTITIONED THEM
 241: (killtable11_20260916/PRETABLE.out, from the close-7 seat's cell-by-cell derivation, opus_close7_20260915/
