 181: ### `crimebankorder` (LOW, device) - the CRIME prelude writes the bank-control registers contiguously; the header interleaves them
 182: 
 183: The prelude's bank loop writes `d->reg[CRIME_MEM_BANK_CTRL0 / sizeof(uint64_t) + bank]` -- CONTIGUOUS offsets
 184: 0x208..0x240 -- while `src/include/thirdparty/crimereg.h:141-148` defines the bank control registers INTERLEAVED (CTRL0
 185: 0x208, CTRL1 0x218, CTRL2 0x210, CTRL3 0x228, CTRL4 0x220, CTRL5 0x238, CTRL6 0x230, CTRL7 0x248; raised by the codex
 186: pass-1 seat on 2026-09-13, re-read 2026-09-26).  If the header is right, banks 1..7 are reported at the wrong offsets
 187: and a guest sizing memory from them reads the wrong layout.  **But the header is itself suspect:** CTRL7 (0x248) is the
 188: same offset as `CRIME_MEM_REFRESH_CNTR` (`:149`), and no bank register sits at 0x240.  Needs a boot witness (the IP32
 189: PROM's memory report, or NetBSD's or OpenBSD's own sizing) before any edit -- the header, not the code, could be wrong.
 190: `#529`'s `top8 anchor` row pins the model's CURRENT layout and says a round re-ordering the banks owes its
 191: re-derivation.  **Called "filed" in the probe since 2026-09-13; filed nowhere until now.**
